[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this carefully.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA partial semigroup $(S,+)$ is a set with a partial binary operation that is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124massociative in the sense that if $(a+b)+c$ and $a+(b+c)$ are both defined, they [0m[38;2;124;124;124mare equal. A commutative partial semigroup means if $a+b$ is defined, then $b+a$[0m[38;2;124;124;124mis defined and equals $a+b$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Stone-Čech compactification $\beta S$ consists of ultrafilters on $S$. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124moperation on $\beta S$ is defined as follows: for ultrafilters $p, q \in \beta [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mS$, the sum $p+q$ is the ultrafilter defined by $A \in p+q$ iff $\{s \in S : \{t[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\in S : s+t \in A\} \in q\} \in p$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, but in a partial semigroup, $s+t$ may not be defined for all pairs. So we [0m[38;2;124;124;124mneed to be more careful.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor a partial semigroup, the operation on $\beta S$ is typically defined as: $A [0m[38;2;124;124;124m\in p+q$ iff $\{s \in S : \{t \in S : s+t \text{ is defined and } s+t \in A\} [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\in q\} \in p$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question is whether this is well-defined as an ultrafilter, and whether the [0m[38;2;124;124;124mresulting structure is a partial semigroup.[0m

